# A VENDORED nano-ros is not part of the consumer's package graph.
#
# `nros-pkg-index` walks a workspace root for `package.xml` and requires the
# names it finds to be unique. nano-ros ships 272 of them (examples, templates,
# fixtures) and they are deliberately NOT unique across the tree: every
# copy-out workspace has its own `demo_bringup`, `talker_pkg`, `zephyr_entry`
# and so on, because each is a separate project that a user copies out whole
# (RFC-0026, RFC-0066). Within one workspace they are unique, which is the only
# scope in which uniqueness means anything.
#
# A consumer that vendors nano-ros as a subdirectory — `nano-ros-rt-eval` does,
# as a submodule — makes their root an ancestor of all 272, and the first
# duplicate ends their build:
#
#     error: nros::main!: build_pkg_index: duplicate pkg name `demo_bringup`
#            in workspace `/…/nano-ros-rt-eval`:
#            `…/nano-ros/examples/templates/c-and-cpp-mixed-workspace/src/demo_bringup`
#        and `…/nano-ros/examples/templates/multi-node-workspace/src/demo_bringup`
#
# naming two directories the consumer does not own, in a repository they merely
# depend on, for a package they never asked to build.
#
# This marker prunes the whole tree from any walk that starts ABOVE it. It does
# NOT affect nano-ros's own discovery: `build_pkg_index` exempts the root it
# was given (`entry.depth() == 0` returns true before any marker is read), so
# when nano-ros IS the workspace root this file is never consulted, and when it
# is nested it prunes at depth 1.
#
# See issue 0621.
